1
00:00:02,545 --> 00:00:04,172
I have a special treat for y'all today.

2
00:00:04,380 --> 00:00:11,137
My dear friend and former college roommate is here from the Johnson space center in Houston to talk to us about our space program.

3
00:00:11,345 --> 00:00:13,306
And yeah, I know what you're thinking.

4
00:00:13,514 --> 00:00:15,057
These guys were roomies.

5
00:00:15,600 --> 00:00:23,024
How does one go on to be a... A fancy scientist at NASA, and the other's teaching freshman science at a public high school?

6
00:00:24,108 --> 00:00:25,108
Yeah, Sheldon.

7
00:00:25,234 --> 00:00:26,819
I was thinking that.

8
00:00:27,904 --> 00:00:28,904
Thank you.

9
00:00:30,239 --> 00:00:35,453
All right, uh, let's give a warm welcome to my good buddy, Dr. Ronald Hodges.

10
00:00:36,662 --> 00:00:37,455
Hey, kids. Hey.

11
00:00:37,663 --> 00:00:45,922
Glad to be here. And, uh, Hubert, the answer to your question is, one of those roomies was busy studying while the other was out chasing high school girls.

12
00:00:48,424 --> 00:00:49,424
Yeah.

13
00:00:49,592 --> 00:00:51,802
To be clear, they were all over 18.

14
00:00:52,011 --> 00:00:53,262
Yeah. Well...

15
00:00:54,263 --> 00:01:00,269
Now, what I thought would be fun to talk about today is what NASA's planning on doing beyond the space shuttle.

16
00:01:00,478 --> 00:01:04,065
Things like the first manned mission to Mars.

17
00:01:04,565 --> 00:01:05,316
Uh, yeah.

18
00:01:05,525 --> 00:01:07,109
Did you see the movie aliens?

19
00:01:07,777 --> 00:01:08,569
I did.

20
00:01:08,778 --> 00:01:10,238
Did you think it was cool?

21
00:01:11,697 --> 00:01:12,697
Uh...

22
00:01:13,616 --> 00:01:14,909
Sure, I enjoyed it.

23
00:01:15,201 --> 00:01:16,202
Me, too.

24
00:01:17,119 --> 00:01:18,371
Football player, right?

25
00:01:18,663 --> 00:01:19,663
Yes, sir.

26
00:01:19,789 --> 00:01:20,957
Yeah.

27
00:01:21,415 --> 00:01:28,297
Now, of course, before we can set out to explore the solar system, we've got a few minor problems to overcome.

28
00:01:28,506 --> 00:01:34,971
Not the least of which is, every time we launch, it costs the U.S. taxpayers hundreds of millions of dollars.

29
00:01:35,805 --> 00:01:36,805
Oh, no, no, no.

30
00:01:37,265 --> 00:01:42,979
Yes. If you want to save money, why don't you land the booster rockets instead of letting them drop in the ocean?

31
00:01:44,981 --> 00:01:48,568
That's a cute idea, but, uh, it's not technically possible.

32
00:01:48,818 --> 00:01:49,902
Why not?

33
00:01:51,862 --> 00:01:53,739
Well, it's hard to explain.

34
00:01:53,948 --> 00:01:55,324
The math is pretty complicated.

35
00:01:55,533 --> 00:01:57,159
Perhaps I could help you with it.

36
00:01:57,827 --> 00:02:00,913
Well, I bet you could.

37
00:02:01,122 --> 00:02:02,748
I'll bet you could.

38
00:02:02,957 --> 00:02:06,252
I'll tell you what. Here is a NASA patch.

39
00:02:06,460 --> 00:02:12,633
Why don't you have your mom sew that on your book bag? Now, who here wants to know how astronauts go to the bathroom?

40
00:02:12,842 --> 00:02:17,555
-Me. I do. Oh, right. See, now, during liftoff, all the astronauts wear diapers.

41
00:02:17,847 --> 00:02:23,269
People often wonder why I chose to pursue a career in theoretical physics.

42
00:02:23,853 --> 00:02:28,816
I usually respond by saying I wanted to unravel the inner workings of the universe.

43
00:02:29,025 --> 00:02:34,614
Yeah, perhaps when captain kirk said, "to boldly go," he meant in one of those, huh?

44
00:02:34,822 --> 00:02:38,701
But the real answer was I wanted to prove this nincompoop wrong.

45
00:02:38,909 --> 00:02:40,620
Yeah, that's right. Beam me up, potty!

46
00:02:42,413 --> 00:02:45,207
♪ nobody else ♪ is stronger than I am

47
00:02:45,416 --> 00:02:48,002
♪ j" yesterday I moved a mountain

48
00:02:48,210 --> 00:02:50,671
♪ j" I bet I could be your hero

49
00:02:50,880 --> 00:02:53,466
♪ j" I am a mighty little man

50
00:02:53,716 --> 00:02:56,969
♪ j" I am a mighty little man.

51
00:03:00,598 --> 00:03:02,391
meemaw: So I saw a lawyer today.

52
00:03:02,808 --> 00:03:03,434
Why?

53
00:03:03,643 --> 00:03:05,936
I'm putting together my last will and testament.

54
00:03:06,395 --> 00:03:07,395
We're gonna miss ya.

55
00:03:07,688 --> 00:03:08,314
George.

56
00:03:08,522 --> 00:03:10,858
Don't worry. I ain't leaving him squat.

57
00:03:11,359 --> 00:03:12,359
Are you dyin'?

58
00:03:12,443 --> 00:03:13,611
We're all dyin', honey.

59
00:03:13,819 --> 00:03:16,947
From the second we're born, it's just a slip and slide into the darkness.

60
00:03:17,239 --> 00:03:19,033
Unless you get bit by a vampire.

61
00:03:19,408 --> 00:03:22,119
Well, now that goes without saying.

62
00:03:24,163 --> 00:03:26,957
I don't want to die. I've only kissed one boy so far.

63
00:03:27,249 --> 00:03:28,249
What?

64
00:03:28,376 --> 00:03:30,378
Relax. It was a long time ago.

65
00:03:31,837 --> 00:03:32,922
Are you relaxed?

66
00:03:33,756 --> 00:03:35,966
Shelly, you haven't touched your dinner.

67
00:03:36,258 --> 00:03:37,301
You feel okay?

68
00:03:37,885 --> 00:03:40,012
Oh, yes. I was just thinking.

69
00:03:40,596 --> 00:03:41,596
About what, baby?

70
00:03:41,847 --> 00:03:45,184
The optimal height-to-width ratio for a reusable rocket.

71
00:03:46,143 --> 00:03:48,646
I was just thinking the exact same thing.

72
00:03:49,188 --> 00:03:50,272
Really?

73
00:03:50,690 --> 00:03:52,400
Oh, moonpie, I love you so much.

74
00:04:15,840 --> 00:04:17,120
It's late. What are you doing up?

75
00:04:17,174 --> 00:04:20,344
I'm working on the hyperbolic calculations for a rocket return.

76
00:04:21,220 --> 00:04:22,220
Need any help?

77
00:04:23,431 --> 00:04:24,431
What?

78
00:04:24,515 --> 00:04:27,643
I'm kiddin'. Now, turn out the light and go to bed.

79
00:04:28,018 --> 00:04:29,061
But... Now.

80
00:04:34,525 --> 00:04:36,444
Dad, can we afford a computer?

81
00:04:37,027 --> 00:04:39,071
You do my taxes. What do you think?

82
00:04:39,780 --> 00:04:41,115
Never mind.

83
00:04:41,782 --> 00:04:43,075
Good night.

84
00:05:13,773 --> 00:05:14,774
Meemaw?

85
00:05:17,818 --> 00:05:20,321
Mr. Moonpie, how might I help you?

86
00:05:20,613 --> 00:05:23,199
How much money are you planning to leave me when you die?

87
00:05:23,407 --> 00:05:24,533
Nice talkin' to you.

88
00:05:26,660 --> 00:05:28,162
Meemaw?

89
00:05:29,914 --> 00:05:31,290
Want to try that again?

90
00:05:31,499 --> 00:05:34,752
I need to buy a computer so I can solve the navier-Stokes equation.

91
00:05:34,960 --> 00:05:37,797
Uh-huh. How much is a computer?

92
00:05:38,005 --> 00:05:42,635
Apple has a nice one for $2,000, but the kind I need costs three million.

93
00:05:43,928 --> 00:05:45,471
Let me see what I got in the vault.

94
00:05:50,184 --> 00:05:53,229
Nine bucks and, uh...

95
00:05:56,065 --> 00:05:58,651
Oh, look at that... a peso.

96
00:06:00,694 --> 00:06:01,821
It's warm.

97
00:06:09,411 --> 00:06:10,663
Hello, first national bank.

98
00:06:10,871 --> 00:06:12,832
I'd like to speak with a loan officer.

99
00:06:13,040 --> 00:06:15,668
It's regarding a second mortgage on my home.

100
00:06:16,335 --> 00:06:18,337
My name is Sheldon Lee Cooper.

101
00:06:19,171 --> 00:06:20,256
Sure, I'll hold.

102
00:06:21,382 --> 00:06:23,592
Oh, I hate hold music.

103
00:06:23,926 --> 00:06:24,926
Hello.

104
00:06:25,010 --> 00:06:27,555
Yes, you can help me, Dorothy Fitzpatrick.

105
00:06:27,930 --> 00:06:30,683
I'm interested in taking out a second mortgage.

106
00:06:31,559 --> 00:06:33,769
I'm nine years old. Why do you ask?

107
00:06:34,687 --> 00:06:39,358
That's called age discrimination, Dorothy, but I'm willing to let it slide.

108
00:06:40,609 --> 00:06:43,612
I'm glad you find me cute, but I'm deadly serious.

109
00:06:43,946 --> 00:06:46,240
I need funds to buy a computer.

110
00:06:46,490 --> 00:06:48,033
No, the house isn't in my name.

111
00:06:48,242 --> 00:06:50,327
I'm nine. We've established this.

112
00:06:51,120 --> 00:06:56,834
I do prepare the taxes for my parents, and if we tighten our belts, we'll have sufficient equity for the loan.

113
00:06:57,042 --> 00:06:58,335
Sheldon, who are you talking to?

114
00:06:59,044 --> 00:07:00,546
Dorothy Fitzpatrick.

115
00:07:00,796 --> 00:07:01,922
Who's Dorothy Fitzpatrick?

116
00:07:02,131 --> 00:07:03,757
Is she a new buddy from school?

117
00:07:04,008 --> 00:07:06,927
She's the loan officer at the first national bank of medford.

118
00:07:07,136 --> 00:07:08,220
Why are you talking to her?

119
00:07:08,804 --> 00:07:09,804
Mom, can this wait?

120
00:07:09,889 --> 00:07:12,016
I'm trying to negotiate favorable terms.

121
00:07:12,600 --> 00:07:14,101
Hello. Dorothy?

122
00:07:19,732 --> 00:07:21,859
Hey, how come you weren't in math class?

123
00:07:22,067 --> 00:07:26,739
That guy from NASA treated me like a child, and I need to prove him wrong.

124
00:07:26,989 --> 00:07:27,989
You are a child.

125
00:07:28,073 --> 00:07:29,825
Tread lightly, my friend.

126
00:07:30,910 --> 00:07:32,870
Was Ms. Ingram upset I wasn't in class?

127
00:07:33,078 --> 00:07:35,122
Actually, she was happy.

128
00:07:35,331 --> 00:07:36,624
She even did a little dance.

129
00:07:36,957 --> 00:07:38,542
Do you know anybody with a modem?

130
00:07:38,751 --> 00:07:40,878
I think radio shack has one. Why?

131
00:07:41,086 --> 00:07:46,342
I need to access thrust-to-weight ratios from the ftp server at the Johnson space center.

132
00:07:46,675 --> 00:07:47,384
Then what?

133
00:07:47,676 --> 00:07:51,680
Then I show that guy from NASA my work and laugh as he begs for mercy.

134
00:07:52,306 --> 00:07:54,516
You know, sometimes you sound like a super villain.

135
00:07:54,725 --> 00:07:55,768
Silence!

136
00:07:56,477 --> 00:07:58,812
That'll be more effective after your voice changes.

137
00:08:01,941 --> 00:08:03,359
Silence.

138
00:08:04,735 --> 00:08:07,029
Mom, can you take me to radio shack?

139
00:08:07,321 --> 00:08:08,322
Not today, baby.

140
00:08:08,530 --> 00:08:11,116
I have to go food shopping and get dinner started.

141
00:08:11,367 --> 00:08:13,160
But it's important.

142
00:08:13,744 --> 00:08:15,663
Sorry. Maybe over the weekend.

143
00:08:15,955 --> 00:08:17,075
But it won't take that long.

144
00:08:17,164 --> 00:08:18,624
Sheldon, I said no.

145
00:08:18,832 --> 00:08:21,418
But, mom... Not another word.

146
00:08:23,337 --> 00:08:24,337
What?

147
00:08:24,755 --> 00:08:26,131
Can't I just be happy?

148
00:08:29,927 --> 00:08:31,095
Mom, please!

149
00:08:31,303 --> 00:08:33,097
Sheldon Lee, I said no.

150
00:08:33,305 --> 00:08:34,473
I'm still happy.

151
00:08:37,393 --> 00:08:39,103
Please, mom! You're being unfair!

152
00:08:39,311 --> 00:08:41,939
No. But I really need those thrust-to-weight ratios.

153
00:08:42,147 --> 00:08:43,524
Do you see I'm cookin'?

154
00:08:43,816 --> 00:08:45,067
Well, take me after dinner.

155
00:08:45,275 --> 00:08:47,736
Enough! I'm not takin' you anywhere tonight.

156
00:08:49,196 --> 00:08:52,908
I'm not proud of it, but as a child, I was prone...

157
00:08:53,158 --> 00:08:54,660
To the occasional meltdown.

158
00:08:54,868 --> 00:08:56,537
Cheese and crackers!

159
00:08:56,870 --> 00:08:59,289
I'm sorry you had to hear that.

160
00:09:04,044 --> 00:09:07,006
All right, I finally got him calmed down.

161
00:09:07,214 --> 00:09:08,507
How'd you manage that?

162
00:09:08,716 --> 00:09:11,719
Easy. Gave him a hug and a little cough syrup.

163
00:09:11,927 --> 00:09:12,594
Mom!

164
00:09:12,803 --> 00:09:15,055
It's not like he's operating heavy machinery.

165
00:09:15,472 --> 00:09:17,641
We can't keep putting up with this behavior.

166
00:09:18,142 --> 00:09:18,934
We need to ground him.

167
00:09:19,143 --> 00:09:22,271
How? If you say "no playing outside," he says, "thank you."

168
00:09:22,479 --> 00:09:23,689
Well, we need to do something.

169
00:09:23,897 --> 00:09:26,191
It's just a tantrum. It's what kids do.

170
00:09:26,400 --> 00:09:27,760
You used to punish me all the time.

171
00:09:27,943 --> 00:09:30,195
That was different. You were a pain in the ass.

172
00:09:31,155 --> 00:09:33,115
I say no radio shack for at least a month.

173
00:09:33,866 --> 00:09:34,575
Sounds fair.

174
00:09:34,783 --> 00:09:35,783
I'll go tell him.

175
00:09:35,951 --> 00:09:37,453
I'd wait and tell him tomorrow.

176
00:09:37,745 --> 00:09:39,329
He's a little loopy right now.

177
00:09:44,001 --> 00:09:45,210
Good night.

178
00:09:46,128 --> 00:09:47,296
She drugged our son.

179
00:09:48,672 --> 00:09:51,842
Thus began the longest month of my life.

180
00:09:54,053 --> 00:10:02,269
Without the ability to access the thrust-to-weight ratios, my well-intended effort to humiliate a NASA scientist ground to a halt.

181
00:10:02,478 --> 00:10:03,478
Mom.

182
00:10:03,562 --> 00:10:05,355
Uh... what's going on?

183
00:10:05,814 --> 00:10:07,608
Something's wrong with Sheldon.

184
00:10:09,777 --> 00:10:12,321
What's the matter, baby? Have a tummy ache?

185
00:10:12,529 --> 00:10:13,822
I think it's an ulcer.

186
00:10:14,198 --> 00:10:14,907
George sr.: Don't be silly.

187
00:10:15,115 --> 00:10:16,825
You must have eaten something. No.

188
00:10:17,034 --> 00:10:19,703
My symptoms are consistent with an ulcer.

189
00:10:20,120 --> 00:10:24,208
Shelly, remember when you thought you had leprosy, and it was just a patch of dry skin?

190
00:10:24,416 --> 00:10:27,211
And when you thought you had gout? Or an enlarged prostate?

191
00:10:27,419 --> 00:10:28,420
Or mad cow?

192
00:10:28,629 --> 00:10:29,880
That was my favorite.

193
00:10:30,089 --> 00:10:32,007
Well, this is different.

194
00:10:33,092 --> 00:10:34,843
Maybe we should take him to the doctor.

195
00:10:36,595 --> 00:10:39,098
Listen to your wife... Ulcers are serious.

196
00:10:39,389 --> 00:10:40,389
Ow.

197
00:10:40,766 --> 00:10:46,522
Well, I've never seen this in someone so young, but, uh, your son has an ulcer.

198
00:10:46,939 --> 00:10:47,939
Thank you.

199
00:10:47,981 --> 00:10:49,233
How could this happen?

200
00:10:49,483 --> 00:10:51,527
Uh, there are a number of factors.

201
00:10:52,027 --> 00:10:55,072
Has he been under some unusual stress lately?

202
00:10:55,280 --> 00:10:57,533
Yes. She won't take me to radio shack.

203
00:10:57,741 --> 00:10:58,951
Go wait outside.

204
00:10:59,201 --> 00:11:03,287
I've heard some interesting research about treating ulcers with antimicrobials.

205
00:11:03,455 --> 00:11:04,455
Go.

206
00:11:06,291 --> 00:11:07,835
And you're surprised I have an ulcer.

207
00:11:08,043 --> 00:11:09,378
I'm surprised I don't have one.

208
00:11:11,964 --> 00:11:13,382
Um, radio shack?

209
00:11:13,799 --> 00:11:16,802
He's trying to figure out how to help NASA land rockets.

210
00:11:17,010 --> 00:11:18,720
Oh. Well, that's a nice thing.

211
00:11:18,929 --> 00:11:20,722
He's just doing it out of spite.

212
00:11:21,974 --> 00:11:23,142
Okay.

213
00:11:23,475 --> 00:11:28,438
Well, keep him off spicy foods for the time being, and I'll write you a prescription for zantac.

214
00:11:28,814 --> 00:11:29,814
Does he smoke?

215
00:11:30,399 --> 00:11:31,900
'Course not. He's nine.

216
00:11:32,109 --> 00:11:33,777
I started at his age.

217
00:11:34,027 --> 00:11:35,779
Uh, but only when I drank.

218
00:11:38,407 --> 00:11:40,993
What the...?

219
00:11:44,037 --> 00:11:45,497
Sheldon! What are you doing?

220
00:11:45,747 --> 00:11:48,292
I just need a minute. These things are so slow.

221
00:11:50,460 --> 00:11:51,712
Is this your family?

222
00:11:52,004 --> 00:11:53,046
They're lovely.

223
00:12:00,095 --> 00:12:01,555
mom. What?

224
00:12:01,889 --> 00:12:03,765
There's something wrong with Sheldon again.

225
00:12:03,974 --> 00:12:04,725
I figured it out!

226
00:12:04,933 --> 00:12:06,643
I figured it out!

227
00:12:07,436 --> 00:12:08,436
One night.

228
00:12:08,520 --> 00:12:09,980
I want to sleep one night.

229
00:12:12,399 --> 00:12:13,442
Oh. Hi, mom.

230
00:12:14,610 --> 00:12:15,610
Hi there.

231
00:12:17,154 --> 00:12:19,406
♪ I'm on my way, ♪ I'm making it...

232
00:12:19,615 --> 00:12:29,666
adult Sheldon: With my research complete and my ulcer on the mend, all that was left to do was send my work to NASA and wait to become america's scientific sweetheart.

233
00:12:29,875 --> 00:12:32,127
♪ and I will walk ♪ through the front door

234
00:12:32,336 --> 00:12:33,336
♪ j" big time...

235
00:12:33,420 --> 00:12:34,564
adult Sheldon: So I waited...

236
00:12:34,588 --> 00:12:36,298
♪ I make it show, yeah

237
00:12:36,506 --> 00:12:38,091
♪ j” big time

238
00:12:38,842 --> 00:12:40,385
and waited...

239
00:12:40,761 --> 00:12:41,553
♪ big time

240
00:12:41,762 --> 00:12:43,222
♪ j” oh, oh, oh...

241
00:12:43,430 --> 00:12:44,514
and waited...

242
00:12:55,275 --> 00:12:56,485
And waited.

243
00:12:59,321 --> 00:13:00,321
♪ j” big.

244
00:13:00,405 --> 00:13:02,407
something's wrong with Sheldon again.

245
00:13:02,616 --> 00:13:03,367
George sr.: Now what?

246
00:13:03,575 --> 00:13:06,370
Those people at NASA never responded to his science stuff.

247
00:13:06,578 --> 00:13:08,914
George sr.: Damn it. I've had enough of this.

248
00:13:09,539 --> 00:13:10,916
Sheldon, get up.

249
00:13:11,124 --> 00:13:12,918
What's the point?

250
00:13:14,336 --> 00:13:15,420
We're going to Houston.

251
00:13:17,047 --> 00:13:18,047
Really?

252
00:13:18,340 --> 00:13:19,340
Yeah.

253
00:13:20,008 --> 00:13:22,928
You and me are gonna give those space monkeys a little talkin' to.

254
00:13:24,263 --> 00:13:32,354
I often found my father to be a strange and puzzling man, but at that moment, I never loved him more.

255
00:13:39,987 --> 00:13:42,030
I don't see why I couldn't stay home.

256
00:13:42,531 --> 00:13:44,950
'Cause we're having a fun family outin'.

257
00:13:45,200 --> 00:13:46,493
Can I at least drive?

258
00:13:46,702 --> 00:13:48,787
She said fun, not tragic.

259
00:13:50,038 --> 00:13:51,540
Let's go to the ostrich farm.

260
00:13:51,748 --> 00:13:54,334
Georgejr; Cool we can ride 'em, and then we can eat 'em.

